86426805 13194139534590 17592186045417 true] [17592186426810 1079 #uuid "3f81bcb6-7113-5591-b120-34386b2b4833" 13194139534590 17592186045417 true] [17592186426811 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426809 13194139534590 17592186045417 true] [17592186426811 1069 "q-36763" 13194139534590 17592186045417 true] [17592186426811 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534590 17592186045417 true] [17592186426811 1071 "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" 13194139534590 17592186045417 true] [17592186426811 1072 #uuid "a278ff29-60c3-57db-888f-d20c4575a799" 13194139534590 17592186045417 true] [17592186426811 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =0.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{2}{3}.$" 13194139534590 17592186045417 true] [17592186426812 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426810 13194139534590 17592186045417 true] [17592186426812 1069 "q-107275" 13194139534590 17592186045417 true] [17592186426812 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534590 17592186045417 true] [17592186426812 1071 "Given that $\\lambda =-5$ and $\\lambda =8$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{aligned}]$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" 13194139534590 17592186045417 true] [17592186426812 1072 #uuid "b7043291-4c2d-5218-b2ec-199ed93eadcb" 13194139534590 17592186045417 true] [17592186426812 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =-5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{aligned}]-(-5)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}+12{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 12 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For $\\lambda =8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{aligned}]-8[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-12{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=6,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534590 17592186045417 true] [17592186426813 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426808 13194139534590 17592186045417 true] [17592186426813 1069 "q-80326" 13194139534590 17592186045417 true] [17592186426813 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534590 17592186045417 true] [17592186426813 1071 "Given that $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda =11$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}]$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" 13194139534590 17592186045417 true] [17592186426813 1072 #uuid "ba90f51b-352b-5787-ba63-3059791890ac" 13194139534590 17592186045417 true] [17592186426813 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 9 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-3{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For $\\lambda =11,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}]-11[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-9{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534590 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-07c3b6eb-d05a-563e-9054-1a09875cbcca" 17592186426793 "answer-0e2a0fd5-5d0b-5043-bea5-3d7c9cdc5bec" 17592186426794 "answer-152f16d1-7717-52d3-80d1-4e93f7e18085" 17592186426795 "answer-22e10d12-85c5-5f27-8869-ab11e85fc4ce" 17592186426796 "answer-4508942a-d2b4-51d0-a2a4-1cfbb3d6e36f" 17592186426797 "answer-48e3bd55-0f69-5377-b778-7910fd7ee930" 17592186426798 "answer-5abf6ee9-af9e-57ac-8483-5a3e973653e7" 17592186426799 "answer-69549ed7-5e3c-59e7-9843-4f93cd72fb1b" 17592186426800 "answer-7b33cfbb-3ab3-5640-a98a-1b640acd1fa3" 17592186426801 "answer-93521080-c7bb-5183-8fdf-d1ef0f91ea9a" 17592186426802 "answer-9624a80a-ce12-59e9-9acd-2860974a182f" 17592186426803 "answer-ae8b7370-66aa-59a2-8b42-8c520344571e" 17592186426804 "answer-e893480f-5916-54cc-a5ac-b814cdf8c446" 17592186426805 "answer-ee78a4db-270a-5a24-94aa-7fd979241cf9" 17592186426806 "answer-f1063d93-3165-53f2-b0c5-7b6d7f584b7e" 17592186426807 "answer-field-1754e42f-ef75-5680-942c-adf751ac0ab2" 17592186426808 "answer-field-354dd761-ee66-534a-8529-b3848bd7316c" 17592186426809 "answer-field-3f81bcb6-7113-5591-b120-34386b2b4833" 17592186426810 "question-a278ff29-60c3-57db-888f-d20c4575a799" 17592186426811 "question-b7043291-4c2d-5218-b2ec-199ed93eadcb" 17592186426812 "question-ba90f51b-352b-57